# `come` and `go` are where the idiom lives: `come a long way`, `go a long way`.
# Any other verb takes `along` as its particle and `way` as an intensifier, so
# "they get along way too well" and "the scenes dragged along way too long" are correct.
expr comeGo [come, comes, came, coming, go, goes, going, went, gone]

expr main <[(@comeGo along way), (along way to go)], (along way)>

let message "Use `a long way`, not `along way`."
let description "Corrects `along way` to `a long way` in `come/go a long way` and `a long way to go`."
let kind "Grammar"
let becomes "a long way"
let strategy "MatchCase"

test "Kevin Rudd has come along way, he has matured and learned about the world." "Kevin Rudd has come a long way, he has matured and learned about the world."
test "I've come along way since those times." "I've come a long way since those times."
test "The tooling for frontend development has come along way too." "The tooling for frontend development has come a long way too."
test "A serious push towards exchanges and wallets supporting bech32 would go along way too." "A serious push towards exchanges and wallets supporting bech32 would go a long way too."
test "I still have along way to go but I'm impressed by my work here." "I still have a long way to go but I'm impressed by my work here."
test "Functional programming goes along way towards voiding the problems of state-derived complexity." "Functional programming goes a long way towards voiding the problems of state-derived complexity."
test "HIS VICTORY AND PRIZE MONEY WILL GO ALONG WAY TOWARD HELPING HIS FAMILY." "HIS VICTORY AND PRIZE MONEY WILL GO A LONG WAY TOWARD HELPING HIS FAMILY."
test "Not without going along way inland." "Not without going a long way inland."
test "This will go along way toward fixing the problem." "This will go a long way toward fixing the problem."
test "We came along way in a short time." "We came a long way in a short time."
test "Harper has come along way since version 1." "Harper has come a long way since version 1."
test "It goes along way to explaining the delay." "It goes a long way to explaining the delay."
test "We still have along way to go before launch." "We still have a long way to go before launch."
test "There is along way to go." "There is a long way to go."
test "Along way to go before this ships." "A long way to go before this ships."
test "A little kindness goes along way." "A little kindness goes a long way."

# `along` is the verb's particle here and `way` is the intensifier, so these are all correct.

allows "These two get along way too well."
allows "We actually get along way better than most."
allows "I'm sure most people would get along way better in person."
allows "Sometimes, the scenes dragged along way too long."
allows "It seemed to be pounding along way too fast."
allows "That module brings along way too many dependencies."
allows "Followed along way too long, and it's astonishing."
allows "The project dragged along way past the deadline."

allows "He came a long way."
allows "We walked along the way we came."

# Accepted gap: `come`/`go` plus intensifier `way`, as in "coming along way faster than
# planned", is still flagged. It is rare next to the misuse; `along way toward` alone
# outnumbers `along way beyond` and `along way past` by two orders of magnitude.
